}, false );
</code></pre>

Q: Insert records in Salesforce sales org causing CANNOT_INSERT_UPDATE_ACTIVATE_ENTITY A: <pre><code>The trigger calls PopulateRating(Trigger.new, Trigger.oldMap) on BeforeInsert — but Trigger.oldMap is always null on insert. Apex can't resolve the overloaded method signature when null is passed as the second argument, hence the error.

**Fix — In the trigger, call the right overload based on context:**apex

if (Trigger.isInsert) {
    AccountTriggerhandler1.PopulateRating(Trigger.new); // single-param overload
} else if (Trigger.isUpdate) {
    AccountTriggerhandler1.PopulateRating(Trigger.new, Trigger.oldMap); // two-param overload
}

The AccountTriggerhandler1 class already has both overloads defined correctly — the trigger just needs to call the right one per context.
Alternatively, consolidate into one method and pass an empty map instead of null on 

insert:
apexAccountTriggerhandler1.PopulateRating(
    Trigger.new, 
    Trigger.isInsert ? new Map&lt;Id,Account&gt;() : Trigger.oldMap
);

The two-param PopulateRating already handles the null/empty old map check with accoldmap == null — so this works cleanly without changing the handler class.

</code></pre>

Q: AutoMapper error: MapperConfiguration does not contain constructor that takes 1 arguments A: <pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var config = new MapperConfiguration(cfg =&gt;
{
    cfg.CreateMap&lt;SectionA, SectionB&gt;();
    cfg.AllowNullDestinationValues = true;
    cfg.AllowNullCollections = true;
}, NullLoggerFactory.Instance);
</code></pre>
<p>You can set a 'NullLoggerFactory' as a second paramter if you don't care about AutoMapper's diagnostic internal logs.</p>

Q: How can I read/write from a flash memory connected through SPI on an embedded Linux system (ARM)? A: <p>There was an error in my <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devicetree" rel="nofollow noreferrer">devicetree</a> table file. The spi1, the image sensor interface (isi1) and the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I2C" rel="nofollow noreferrer">I²C</a> (i2c1) were using the same pins. When compiling the kernel and the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devicetree#Example" rel="nofollow noreferrer">DTS</a> file, there aren’t any errors shown.</p>
<p>In general, the combination of fdisk + mtd_debug was very useful to check the driver and the hardware at low level. In my case, SAMA5D35 and own mainboard @ dts:</p>
<pre><code>ahb {
    apb {
        spi1: spi@f8008000 {
            cs-gpios = &lt;&amp;pioC 25 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW&gt;;
            status = &quot;okay&quot;;
            m25p80@0 {
                compatible = &quot;spansion,s25fl132k&quot;, &quot;jedec,spi-nor&quot;;
                spi-max-frequency = &lt;108000000&gt;;
                reg = &lt;0&gt;;
                m25p,fast-read;
            };
        };

        // Conflicts with spi1
        i2c1: i2c@f0018000 {
            status = &quot;disabled&quot;;
        };
        // Conflicts with spi1 (pin PC27 peripheral B TWCK1 pin, and conflicts with SPI1_NPCS2, ISI_D10)
        isi: isi@f0034000 {
            status = &quot;disabled&quot;;
        };
};
</code></pre>
<p>Now it works fine.</p>

Q: How can I get thumbnails of a website from its URL? A: <p>The two existing answers are both dead now: PhantomJS is archived, and the goingup/thumboo service in your question is gone. The current way to turn a URL into a thumbnail is to render it in headless Chrome and resize the result. With Node, Playwright plus sharp does the whole thing:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const { chromium } = require('playwright');
const sharp = require('sharp');

async function thumbnail(url, out, w = 200, h = 150) {
  const browser = await chromium.launch();
  const page = await browser.newPage({ viewport: { width: 1280, height: 720 } });
  await page.goto(url, { waitUntil: 'networkidle' });
  const full = await page.screenshot();         // PNG buffer of the viewport
  await browser.close();
  await sharp(full).resize(w, h, { fit: 'cover' }).toFile(out);
}

thumbnail('https://www.google.com', 'thumb.png');
</code></pre>
<p><code>fit: 'cover'</code> keeps the aspect ratio and crops, which is what you want for a thumbnail. If you need the whole page instead of just the fold, pass <code>{ fullPage: true }</code> to <code>page.screenshot()</code> before resizing.</p>
<p>If you do not want to run and keep a headless browser alive (it is the part that breaks on servers, in lambdas, and when sites sit behind Cloudflare), a hosted screenshot API takes a URL and returns the image directly. ScreenshotRender works this way over a single GET, returns a hosted image URL, and strips cookie banners before the shot, which matters for clean thumbnails.</p>
